---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Rawan Aladdin — For Loblaw Digital"
  description="A targeted mini-portfolio: design systems, product UX, inclusive design and shipped work by Rawan Aladdin, for the Loblaw Digital Designer role."
  bodyClass="ld-pitch"
  canonical="https://rawanaladdin.com/loblaw-digital"
  noindex
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/loblaw-digital.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <main>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ HERO ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="hero" class="ld-section" style="min-height: 100vh; justify-content: flex-end; padding-bottom: 8rem;">
      <p class="ld-hero-label">For Loblaw Digital — August 2026</p>
      <h1 class="ld-hero-h1">Hi Loblaw<br>Digital, I'm Rawan.</h1>
      <p class="ld-hero-sub">
        A designer who writes the thing down — the persona, the architecture, the
        contrast floor, the usage ratios — and then goes and lands it in the product
        herself. Systems, interfaces, and the visual personality that carries them.
      </p>
      <div class="ld-hero-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════ THE MATRIX ═══════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="map" class="ld-section">
      <p class="ld-section-label">Your posting, answered</p>
      <h2 class="ld-h2">Six disciplines,<br>six artefacts.</h2>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        You named six practice areas. Here is the specific thing I made for each
        one, and where on this page to find it.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-map">

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Problem discovery<br>&amp; goal setting</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            Interviews, surveys and competitor analysis on <strong>Onward</strong>, turned
            into a primary persona and three design pillars that every later call was
            checked against. The brief that came out of it — <em>build confidence without
            adding pressure</em> — is what made the hard decision decidable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Interaction design</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            Onward's information architecture and user flows, shaped around the persona's
            journey, plus the interaction calls that came out of it: the split timer, the
            STAR scaffolding, and filler-word feedback that waits until you've finished
            speaking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Art direction<br>&amp; visual design</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            <strong>Feeds</strong> — a mark, a wordmark, a palette and a set of textures for a
            security tool that had no visual personality at all, then carried across a
            product UI, a digest email, a marketing site, print and motion for a launch
            booth. One tone, five surfaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Design systems<br>thinking</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            Eleven pages of Feeds guidelines — a five-step type scale, fifteen icons drawn
            to one grammar, palette ratios, four explicit don'ts, an AA contrast floor — and
            then I applied them into the product myself, in the engineer's codebase, through
            his code review. That review is where the <strong>shared naming</strong> actually
            got settled.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Research, validation<br>&amp; iteration</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            Onward usability-tested with twelve users at 8/10 for ease of use. What they
            wanted changed was the feedback flow, and so we redesigned it before launch. On
            in-market KPIs I'm short of what you're asking for, and I've said so plainly
            below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="ld-map-row">
          <p class="ld-map-k">Inclusive design</p>
          <p class="ld-map-v">
            What AODA requires of web content is WCAG 2.0 Level AA — the floor I've built to
            since my BCIT placement, where I worked with developers on responsive
            WCAG-compliant interfaces. The grey areas are further down — six things I fixed
            on my own site that no law required.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ PROJECT 01 — FEEDS ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="feeds" class="ld-section">
      <p class="ld-section-label">Project 01 — A design system that shipped</p>
      <div class="ld-project-head">
        <h2 class="ld-project-title">Feeds</h2>
        <span class="ld-meta">Brand Identity · Product UI · 2026</span>
      </div>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        A self-hosted reader and digest engine for security analysts. I didn't write the
        engine — an engineer I work with did — but more or less everything you can see,
        I made.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-tags">
        <span class="ld-tag">Design System</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Guidelines</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Art Direction</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Product UI</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Accessibility</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Front-end</span>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-spec">
        <div>
          <div class="ld-spec-n">11</div>
          <div class="ld-spec-l">Pages of<br>guidelines</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-spec-n">5</div>
          <div class="ld-spec-l">Type steps,<br>each specified</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-spec-n">15</div>
          <div class="ld-spec-l">Custom icons,<br>one grammar</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-spec-n">AA</div>
          <div class="ld-spec-l">Contrast floor,<br>written in</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">01 — The system</p>
        <h3>Specified down to the ratio.</h3>
        <p>
          Five colours, each carrying the proportion of a page it should occupy — paper
          50%, ink 28%, sage 15%, the tints the remaining 7%. A five-step type scale in
          Bricolage Grotesque, from a 108px display down to a 21px caption, each step named
          for what it's for. Fifteen custom icons sharing one grammar: 2px strokes, no fill,
          rounded joins, drawn on a consistent grid, with a rule that new icons join the
          family before anyone reaches for a stock set.
        </p>
        <p>
          The document also names four explicit don'ts: never stretch the mark, never
          recolour it outside the palette, never rotate it, and never place it where
          contrast drops below AA.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-decision">
        <p class="ld-decision-k">Who the document was written for</p>
        <p>
          One reader: the developer building the product. That changes what goes in it.
          Everything in there is something he can hold his own work up against while he's
          in a hurry — a ratio, a pixel value, a thing never to do. I left the adjectives
          out.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-media-grid">
        <div class="ld-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-05.webp" alt="Page 5 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Logo Usage. Three approved lockup combinations on paper, ink and sage, above four don'ts: never stretch, never recolour outside the palette, never place where contrast drops below AA, never rotate." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="ld-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-06.webp" alt="Page 6 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Color Palette. Five swatches with hex and RGB values and a stated role for each, above a usage-ratio bar reading paper 50 percent, ink 28 percent, sage 15 percent, sage dark and light 7 percent." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="ld-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-07.webp" alt="Page 7 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Typography. A five-step type scale in Bricolage Grotesque from 108px display to 21px caption, each with weight and purpose, plus the full weight range and the variable font's axes." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="ld-media">
          <img src="/feeds/guidelines-08.webp" alt="Page 8 of the Feeds brand guidelines — Iconography. Fifteen custom icons on a consistent grid, from Feed and AI to Phishing and Monitoring, all drawn with 2px strokes, no fill and rounded joins." loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="ld-caption">Four of the eleven pages: logo usage and the don'ts, the palette with its ratios, the type scale, and the icon set.</p>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">02 — Into the product</p>
        <h3>I applied it myself, and took the code review.</h3>
        <p>
          The interface, the icon set, the Smart Inbox navigation — written in CSS and
          JavaScript, inside his repository, reviewed like anyone else's. Some of the UX
          calls came out of that back-and-forth: moving Run Audit inside the digest card,
          simplifying its toggle, fixing empty-list CSS bugs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-decision">
        <p class="ld-decision-k">Why this is the part I'd argue for</p>
        <p>
          A design system only becomes real at the moment someone else uses it without
          asking you. Being the one who lands it means the naming gets settled in both
          directions, by two people looking at the same component in the same file. And it
          means I know what a component costs before I propose it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-media-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="ld-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-ui-articles.webp" alt="The Feeds article list after the brand refresh, showing inline CVE, malware and actor chips extracted locally at view time." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="ld-caption">The product UI after the refresh — I wrote this front-end.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-booth.webp" alt="The Feeds booth during the showcase — table runners carrying the mark and tagline, a laptop looping the brand identity reel, and the live product site on the screen behind." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="ld-caption">The launch booth, printed from the same brand kit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="ld-note">
        Worth being precise about ownership: the Go engine, the architecture and the core
        product logic are the engineer's. The identity, the guidelines, the applied
        front-end, the email, the marketing site and the launch are mine.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-cta-links">
        <a href="/feeds.html" class="main-link">read the full case study &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ PROJECT 02 — ONWARD ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="onward" class="ld-section">
      <p class="ld-section-label">Project 02 — Discovery through to shipped</p>
      <div class="ld-project-head">
        <h2 class="ld-project-title">Onward</h2>
        <span class="ld-meta">Product Designer &amp; Lead Developer · Sept – Dec 2024</span>
      </div>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        An AI interview coach for internationally educated healthcare professionals —
        nurses, mostly, arriving into a hiring system that expects them to interview in a
        register nobody teaches them. I ran the research, designed it, and led the build.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-tags">
        <span class="ld-tag">User Research</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Personas</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Information Architecture</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">User Flows</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Product UI</span>
        <span class="ld-tag">Usability Testing</span>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">01 — Discovery</p>
        <h3>The struggle wasn't qualification. It was communication.</h3>
        <p>
          We started by listening — interviews and surveys with internationally educated
          healthcare workers. A clear pattern came back: not language alone, but
          confidence, cultural context, and the quiet fear of being misunderstood by a
          system that wasn't built with you in mind.
        </p>
        <p>
          That became <strong>Marco Dizon</strong>, the primary persona: a nurse from the
          Philippines with years of clinical experience who falters in Canadian interviews.
          Technically overqualified, emotionally underconfident. That combination was the
          design brief.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">02 — Framing</p>
        <h3>How do you build confidence without adding pressure?</h3>
        <p>
          Most interview prep tools are high-stakes simulations, so they amplify the anxiety
          they were meant to relieve. Marco needed a sandbox instead — a place to practise,
          fail safely, get specific feedback, and go again. <strong>Growth</strong>,
          <strong>confidence</strong> and <strong>communication</strong> became the three
          pillars, and every later call got checked against them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">03 — Structure</p>
        <h3>An architecture shaped around his journey.</h3>
        <p>
          Understand the role he's targeting, practise with tailored questions, get concrete
          feedback, review and iterate. The timeline was tight, so we prioritised the
          practice-interview flow — the one Marco would return to most — and accepted that
          everything else would follow it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-media-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="ld-media">
            <img src="/PersonaPrimary.webp" alt="Marco Dizon, the primary persona — a nurse from the Philippines with years of clinical experience, profiled with goals, frustrations and interview anxieties." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="ld-caption">Marco Dizon — built from the interviews, not from a template.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-media">
            <img src="/sitemap.webp" alt="Onward's information architecture and user flows, mapping the journey from awareness through practice to review." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="ld-caption">The architecture, mapped onto his journey through the product.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">04 — The decision</p>
        <h3>The feedback waits until he has finished.</h3>
        <p>
          The system detects filler words in real time. The obvious move was to flag
          them — tell someone the instant they say "um."
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-decision">
        <p class="ld-decision-k">The call I'd defend</p>
        <p>
          I sat with that for a while and went the other way. Someone rehearsing for a
          high-stakes interview does not need a machine scoring them mid-sentence. So the
          detection became a pattern they read back afterward, in their own speech, next to
          STAR-method scaffolding — and the timer was split, counting up while they practise
          and down only when they ask for a real mock. Every step had to say
          <em>you're improving</em>, or the product worked against its own brief.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-block">
        <p class="ld-block-n">05 — Validation</p>
        <h3>Twelve users, and one thing they told us to fix.</h3>
        <p>
          Ease of use came back at 8 out of 10. What they wanted changed was the feedback
          flow, and so we redesigned it before launch.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-stats">
        <div>
          <div class="ld-stat-n">8/10</div>
          <div class="ld-stat-l">Ease of use, 12 users</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-stat-n">4</div>
          <div class="ld-stat-l">Months to shipped</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="ld-stat-n">200+</div>
          <div class="ld-stat-l">Presented to, live</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ld-media">
        <img src="/ONWARD-main.webp" alt="The Onward dashboard — practice and mock interview entry points, saved resumes, and interview history." loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <p class="ld-note">
        Onward was pitched to VANTEC Network angel investors and presented live to a room
        of professionals, investors and government officials. It was a team project; the
        product design, the design system, the research and the pitch were mine.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-cta-links">
        <a href="https://onward-p2.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">see it running &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/onward.html" class="main-link">read the full case study &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ INCLUSIVE DESIGN ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="inclusive" class="ld-section">
      <p class="ld-section-label">Inclusive design</p>
      <h2 class="ld-h2">The half<br>nobody checks.</h2>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        What AODA actually requires of web content is conformance with WCAG 2.0 Level AA.
        That's a floor, and it's the one I've been building to since my BCIT placement,
        where I worked with developers to ship responsive WCAG-compliant interfaces.
        Everything past that floor is where the design work actually is.
      </p>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        I audited this site's own code this month. Across six stylesheets there were zero
        focus styles and zero reduced-motion handling, and the whole page was hidden until
        JavaScript loaded. Here is what changed — none of it a legal requirement:
      </p>

      <ul class="ld-check">
        <li>
          <strong>The site no longer needs JavaScript to be visible.</strong> It was rendering
          <code>display: none</code> on the body until a script fired, which is a blank page for
          anyone whose script didn't.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A visible focus ring, everywhere.</strong> There were no
          <code>:focus-visible</code> rules at all, so keyboard users had no way to tell where
          they were.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A global reduced-motion block.</strong> The homepage had a five-second
          staggered entrance animation that ran regardless of what the operating system had
          been told.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A menu that behaves like a menu.</strong> One shared component with a real
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, the closed panel marked
          <code>inert</code> so it's out of the tab order, Escape to close, and focus returned to
          the trigger. The About page had the panel but no trigger at all.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A live region on the project filters.</strong> The pills now carry
          <code>aria-pressed</code>, and the result count is announced when it changes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> per page, and alt text that says something.</strong>
          Several pages had none, or several.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="ld-note">
        The reason this is on a job application: a checker will find the AA floor for you.
        Focus return, reduced motion and announced state changes only ever get fixed if
        someone goes looking — and going looking is the design decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════ ON MEASURING ═══════════════════════ -->
    <section id="measuring" class="ld-section">
      <p class="ld-section-label">On measurement</p>
      <h2 class="ld-h2">The honest<br>version.</h2>

      <div class="ld-honest">
        <p class="ld-honest-k">What I can't evidence yet</p>
        <p>
          Your posting asks for the key performance indicators I used to measure my
          solutions, and for how I tracked work post-launch. I'm short there. What I've
          measured is what I could reach: twelve users, 8 out of 10 on ease of use, and a
          feedback flow redesigned because of what they said. I have not yet had a product
          in front of millions of people with a dashboard behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the thing I want from a next role, and it's most of why this one
          interests me.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="ld-lede" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        What I'd bring in the meantime is the habit underneath it. On Feeds, the system was
        written so that conformance to it is checkable: every rule in it is a ratio, a pixel
        value or a number someone can hold a design up against. On Onward, the three pillars
        existed so a disagreement about an interaction could be settled by asking which
        pillar it served. Both come from the same instinct as a KPI — decide in advance what
        would count as the thing working.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="ld-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════ ABOUT / CTA ═══════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="about" class="ld-section" style="min-height: 90vh;">
      <p class="ld-section-label">About</p>
      <h2 class="ld-h2">Where I am,<br>plainly.</h2>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        I'm in Vancouver and open to relocating to Toronto. I lived there from 2021 to 2023,
        studying cognitive science, philosophy and digital humanities at the University of
        Toronto and directing photo and video for a student association, so moving back
        would be familiar ground. I hold a Bachelor of Creative Industries and a Diploma in
        Digital Design &amp; Development, both from BCIT.
      </p>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        I'm earlier in my career than the upper end of what you've written down. I'll say
        that plainly. What I have is work that shipped and got used: a product tested with
        real users and redesigned on what they said, a design system living in someone
        else's repository, and clients whose businesses depended on the thing being right.
      </p>
      <p class="ld-lede">
        I use Claude daily in my practice — for research, for building, for pressure-testing
        my own decisions. It's fastest at compressing the distance between a question and
        something I can look at. Deciding which question was worth asking is still on me.
      </p>

      <div class="ld-cta-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/rawan-aladdin" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">linkedin &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="ld-footnote">
        Made for Loblaw Digital — August 2026. Built in Astro, by me.
      </p>
    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
